  "verification": {
    "status": "unverified",
    "needs_teacher_verification": true,
    "note": "Sourced from the Sri Chaitanya Junior Fastrack zoology section (book p.38, SAQ 33). Zoology holds only ONE source book — the TSBIE Basic Learning Material in hand covers physics only, and no zoology board paper is in the corpus — so the two-book union check and the back-test are both structurally impossible for this card; 'not checked' does NOT mean 'checked and clean'. The mark split is a claim until a Telangana IPE teacher confirms it. BOOK GAP: the book's printed FROG HEART figure labels ten structures (carotid arch, systemic arch, pulmocutaneous arch, right and left precaval, right and left atrium, conus arteriosus, ventricle, post caval) but does NOT label the sinus venosus, although its own written answer describes it. The card's figure adds the sinus venosus, drawn as a broken outline because it lies on the dorsal side and is hidden in a ventral view. NAMING: the book and NCERT both call the outlet the conus arteriosus; many texts call the same tube the truncus arteriosus, so the card gives both names once. FIGURE SCOPE: the card's figure is a simplified VENTRAL view. Three points where it departs from a full anatomical drawing, all for legibility (the renderer draws outlines only, so nothing can be hidden behind anything): (1) the three aortic arches are drawn on ONE side and the other trunk is cut short — the written answer says each trunk gives the same three; (2) the conus arteriosus is drawn rising in the midline lane between the two auricles, whereas in the animal it arises towards the animal's right and crosses the ventral face obliquely; (3) the postcaval is cut short below the ventricle because it runs up BEHIND it and is not seen from the front. The sinus venosus is drawn as a broken outline, the ordinary hidden-line convention, because it is dorsal."
  },
