      {
        "id": "s1_chambers",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Position and chambers",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Chambers",
        "margin_note": "Position, chamber count, pericardium. Three or four lines is enough for this mark.",
        "lines": [
          "The heart of a frog is a muscular pump. It lies",
          "in the upper part of the body cavity.",
          "It is three chambered.",
          "There are two auricles (atria) and one",
          "ventricle.",
          "A double layered pericardium surrounds the",
          "heart. The fluid between its two layers",
          "protects the heart from mechanical shock."
        ],
        "why": "Three chambers is the fact the whole answer is built on: one ventricle for two auricles is what makes the frog's circulation incomplete at the end of the answer.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing four chambers. The frog has two auricles and only ONE ventricle.",
          "Calling the pericardium a single membrane. It is double layered, with fluid between the layers."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Two in, one out: two auricles pour into one ventricle."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_sinus_venosus",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Sinus venosus",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Sinus venosus",
        "margin_note": "Shape, side, the three veins, and where it opens. Four short lines.",
        "lines": [
          "The sinus venosus is a triangular thin walled",
          "chamber.",
          "It lies on the DORSAL side of the heart.",
          "Three major veins pour blood into it: the right",
          "precaval, the left precaval and the postcaval.",
          "The sinus venosus opens into the right auricle."
        ],
        "why": "The sinus venosus is the collecting chamber, so it is described by the three veins that empty into it and the one chamber it empties into.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Placing the sinus venosus on the ventral side. It is DORSAL; the conus arteriosus is the ventral one.",
          "Writing that it opens into the ventricle. It opens into the RIGHT AURICLE."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Dorsal side collects, ventral side sends out: sinus venosus behind, conus arteriosus in front."
      },
